codeSplitter merge: settle an edit to a statement the other side joined or wrapped (#1256)
Closes #1256.

**What changes.** A stretch both sides reshaped that `blend` can't settle line by line gets one more try, `rejoin`. It applies when one side joined lines or wrapped one line, and the other side only edited the same lines (same ids, no lines added or removed). It reads each side's lines as one text and merges them word by word with `blendLine`. The result keeps the line breaks of the side that moved them.

Guards:
- No more than four lines a side, so it stays one statement and avoids the misaligned braces §1 rejected whole-hunk word merges for.
- In code, the #1168 rule still app

codeSplitter merge: both sides wrap one expression in && / || and the word merge changes its grouping, silently
Found by the merge replay of 14 hand-fixed "merge main" commits (`apps/codesplitter/docs/merge-solutions.md` §10). Two silent bad merges, the worst outcome the replay has: the file settles with no card, typechecks, and does something else.

**What happens.** Base has a plain expression. Each side wraps it in a new `&&` / `||` with its own parentheses. The word merge splices the two sets of new tokens into one line, and the grouping comes out different from both sides. #1168 cards a stretch where both sides edit the links of a `? :` or `&&` chain that base already had. Here base had no chain, so it doesn't apply.

**#1189 `137baa3`, `apps/git-recon/src/ui/scene.ts`**

```ts
// base
fig.root.visible = s.alive || b.down < SURRENDER + SINK;
// main
fig.root.visible = !s.hidden && !s.gone && (s.alive || b.down < SURRENDER + SINK);
// branch
fig.root.visible = (s.alive || b.down < SURRENDER + SINK) && ride?.kind !== "heli";
// codeSplitter
fig.root.visible = !s.hidden && !(s.gone && (s.alive || b.down < SURRENDER + SINK)) && ride?.kind !== "heli";
// the fix
fig.root.visible = !s.hidden && !s.gone && (s.alive || b.down < SURRENDER + SINK) && ride?.kind !== "heli";
```

